Title: Bug: (react-dom): Memory leak in Fizz Server due to unremoved AbortSignal listeners in Web Streams

Body: React version: 19.1.0-canary (Latest main branch)

There is a memory leak vulnerability in the React DOM Fizz Server (`ReactDOMFizzServerNode.js`) when utilizing `renderToReadableStream` and `resume` for web streams. Currently, when an `AbortSignal` is passed through options, an event listener is attached to the signal to abort the stream request if necessary. 

However, when the stream completes successfully or terminates due to an error, the `abortListener` is never cleared. This keeps a reference to the request context and stream handlers alive in memory, preventing garbage collection on long-lived `AbortSignal` references.

## Steps To Reproduce

1. Pass a shared or long-lived `AbortSignal` into `renderToReadableStream` options across multiple SSR requests.
2. Observe that every single request appends a new listener to the signal that stays attached indefinitely even after the stream is fully closed or finished.
3. This leads to a continuous build-up of memory under heavy traffic, causing a severe memory leak in Node.js/Edge environments.



Link to code example:

Inside `packages/react-dom/src/server/ReactDOMFizzServerNode.js`, the abort listener is attached like this but lacks any cleanup logic during the stream's lifecycle completion:

```javascript
if (options && options.signal) {
 const signal = options.signal;
 if (signal.aborted) {
 abort(request, (signal as any).reason);
 } else {
 abortListener = () => {
 abort(request, (signal as any).reason);
 };
 signal.addEventListener('abort', abortListener);
 }
}
The current behavior
When renderToReadableStream or resume finishes processing (either successfully via onAllReady or due to errors via onShellError/onFatalError), the abort event listener remains attached to the AbortSignal. If the signal is reused or lives longer than the request, it leaks memory indefinitely.

The expected behavior
The abortListener should be cleanly removed using signal.removeEventListener('abort', abortListener) as soon as the stream reaches a terminal state (onAllReady, onShellError, or onFatalError) to allow proper garbage collection.

Title: [Compiler Bug]: parameters or variables in nested functions can cause those functions to be misidentified as uninitialized

Body: ### What kind of issue is this?

- [x] React Compiler core (the JS output is incorrect, or your app works incorrectly after optimization)
- [ ] babel-plugin-react-compiler (build issue installing or using the Babel plugin)
- [ ] eslint-plugin-react-hooks (build issue installing or using the eslint plugin)
- [ ] react-compiler-healthcheck (build issue installing or using the healthcheck script)

### Link to repro

[URL]

### Repro steps

When a nested function defines a parameter or local variable with the same name as the function, the compiler gets confused into thinking that the function is uninitialized.
 [CODE_BLOCK] export default function Greetings() {
 // A parameter or local variable with the same name as the function triggers the bug
 function greeting(greeting) {
 return "Hello " + greeting;
 }

 return {greeting()} ;
 // ^^^^^^^^ this is uninitialized
 // Invariant: [InferMutationAliasingEffects] Expected value kind to be initialized
}
 [/CODE_BLOCK] 
also triggers with a local variable instead of a parameter:
 [CODE_BLOCK] function greeting() {
 let greeting = "Hello world";
 return greeting;
 }
 [/CODE_BLOCK] 
A function in the global scope does not trigger the error, nor does an arrow function like `const greeting = (..) => {..}`.

The context and error message are similar to #37058, but this is triggered by a parameter or local variable, not a recursive reference to the function. If they share the same root cause, then take this bug report as a suggestion for additional regression tests.

The error happens both on the latest compiler version on the playground, but also on oxlint 1.80.0, which uses a fork of the upcoming rust port.

### How often does this bug happen?

Every time

### What version of React are you using?

19.2

### What version of React Compiler are you using?

latest on playground

